+# ADR-0012: The view is always the user's choice
+
+## Status
+Accepted (2026-11-09)
+
+Partly supersedes [ADR-0005](0005-view-registry.md): `AUTO`, `autoMinWidth`, the settings entry `Auto`, and the consequence that a new view cannot skip the contract scenarios. Partly supersedes [ADR-0002](0002-model-presenter-swappable-views.md): `viewMode` has no `AUTO`, and the view is never chosen by width.
+
+## Context
+
+An automatic view chosen by container width makes the app unpredictable: the view changes when a window is resized or a phone is turned. The owner decided that the view is always the user's choice. The change `add-view-mode-setting-and-grid-skeleton` adds the second view, Grid, and the setting that chooses between Cards and Grid.
+
+## Decision
+
+- There is no `AUTO` mode and no minimum width per view. The width and the orientation never choose a view.
+- The stored view mode is a registered view id or none. None, and an id that no registered view has, show Cards. An unregistered id does not make the preferences document unreadable.
+- The settings list the registered views, in registry order, by name; a new view appears there by itself.
+- The view contract selects each view through the stored view mode, not through the viewport.
+- A view-contract scenario that a view cannot pass yet, because a part of its design is not built, may be excluded for that view only, by a pending tag that names the view and the change that re-enables it (today `@grid-pending-hour-cells` on "Select a time"). Every other view still runs it.
+
+## Consequences
+Positive:
+- The app behaves the same at every width and orientation.
+- A view that is still being built can be registered and developed in parts without breaking the contract of the other views.
+
+Negative:
+- A narrow screen can show a view that fits it badly; the user changes it in the settings.
+- A pending tag can be forgotten; each one names the change that removes it.
+
+## Alternatives Considered
+**Keep `AUTO` as the default**: the view still changes by itself, which is what the owner rejected.
+**Choose by device type**: the same unpredictability, and a window on a desktop is as narrow as a phone.
